Turn on R8 in a release build with an older version of the Appwrite Android SDK and nothing fails at compile time. The build succeeds, the app launches, and then the problems start. Database queries reach the server as `{}`. Realtime subscriptions connect but never deliver an event. Users who install your next release find themselves signed out.

Until now, the workaround was to leave R8 off or write your own keep rules for the SDK. That stops being an option in February 2027, when Google Play starts requiring a minimum level of code optimization for apps with more than 10 MB of DEX code.

**Appwrite Android SDK 29.0.0 works with R8 full mode.** Queries, operators, Realtime, and saved sessions behave the same in a shrunk release build as they do in debug. The SDK ships the R8 rules it needs, and those rules are narrow enough to leave R8 room to optimize. Here's what broke, why, and what to change in your own app.

# What changed in Android SDK 29.0.0

Version 29.0.0 of the [Android SDK](/docs/quick-starts/android) makes these R8 changes:

- **Queries and operators** serialize to correct JSON under R8 instead of `{}`.
- **Realtime** delivers events in shrunk builds, and Realtime errors keep their message and code.
- **Session cookies** are saved with fixed key names, so a new build of your app can still read them.
- **Narrower keep rules** for the bundled MQTT client lift a minimal R8 app's obfuscation score from 67.4% to 89.3% and its optimization score from 65.8% to 87.8%.
- **R8 coverage in CI**: the SDK's end-to-end test suite now runs against R8-shrunk code.

There's nothing to configure. The SDK ships consumer R8 rules, and the only code you might need to touch is your own model classes, covered later in this post. The [changelog entry](/changelog/entry/2026-10-04) has the short version.

# Google Play's DEX optimization requirement

Google [announced in August 2026](https://android-developers.googleblog.com/2026/08/app-quality-memory-optimization-secure-onboarding.html) that, starting in February 2027, apps on Google Play must be "optimized with a minimum of 25% coverage across optimization, shrinking, and obfuscation." Apps and games that miss the threshold "may see reduced app visibility and publishing capabilities on Google Play."

Google's [DEX code optimization](https://developer.android.com/google/play/vitals/code-optimization) page sets the scope. Android vitals calculates these metrics for every app, but only alerts apps whose bundle contains at least 10 MB of uncompressed DEX code, or 50 MB for games. Any shrinker counts. For most Android teams, that means R8.

Two details make this a library problem as well as an app problem:

- **Play reads the scores from your bundle.** For apps built with AGP 8.10 or later, the percentages come from the `r8.json` file R8 embeds in the app bundle. They describe the final build, every dependency included.
- **Library keep rules become your keep rules.** Consumer rules shipped inside a library's AAR merge into your app's R8 configuration. A broad keep rule in a dependency stops R8 from renaming or optimizing that code, and the cost shows up in your app's scores.

An SDK that breaks under R8 pushes you to turn R8 off. An SDK with blanket keep rules lowers your scores even with R8 on. Version 29.0.0 addresses both.

# Why R8 full mode broke the Android SDK

[R8 full mode](https://developer.android.com/topic/performance/app-optimization/enable-app-optimization) has been the default since Android Gradle Plugin 8.0. It makes stricter assumptions about reflection than ProGuard compatibility mode. If R8 can't see your code use a class, constructor, or field, it's free to remove, rename, or restructure it.

Earlier versions of the SDK relied on **Gson reflection** in a few internal paths. Gson reads and writes fields by name at runtime, which R8 can't trace. Each of the three bugs below comes from that gap, and each matches a failure mode in [Gson's troubleshooting guide](https://github.com/google/gson/blob/main/Troubleshooting.md).

## Queries and operators serialized to `{}`

The SDK turns `Query` and `Operator` values into JSON before they go to the API. It did this by handing the object to Gson, which reflected over its `method`, `attribute`, and `values` fields. Under R8, Gson found nothing to serialize, and every query reached Appwrite as an empty object.

That affected every list call that took [queries](/docs/products/databases/tablesdb/queries): filters, pagination, ordering, and the nested `Query.and`, `Query.or`, and `Query.elemMatch` helpers. [Operators](/docs/products/databases/tablesdb/operators) for atomic updates had the same problem.

## Realtime connected but delivered nothing

[Realtime](/docs/apis/realtime) parses each WebSocket message into a `RealtimeResponse` before routing it. That class was only ever created by Gson, never by SDK code, so R8 full mode treated it as never instantiated and made it abstract. Every incoming message threw `Abstract classes can't be instantiated`. The socket stayed open and subscription callbacks never fired.

Realtime errors failed more quietly. The SDK cast error payloads into an `AppwriteException` through Gson, and under R8 the exception arrived with a `null` message and code. Your error handler ran, but it had nothing to log.

## Saved sessions broke after an app update

The Android SDK persists session cookies in `SharedPreferences` so users stay signed in across launches. It wrote them by serializing an internal cookie class with Gson. Once R8 renamed that class's fields, cookies were saved with obfuscated keys like `{"c":…,"d":…}` instead of `name`, `value`, and `domain`.

Obfuscated names aren't stable across builds. When you shipped a new version and the mapping changed, the new build couldn't read the cookies the old one had saved, and users lost their session on update. This is the hardest of the three to catch, because it only shows up when one release build reads data written by another.

## How the SDK fixes it: explicit maps instead of reflection

The fix is the same in all three places. Instead of letting Gson reflect over SDK classes, the SDK now converts them to and from plain maps with keys written in code. R8 can rename fields as much as it likes, because the JSON keys are string literals.

Here's the new serialization path for `Query`:

```kotlin
fun toMap(): Map<String, Any?> = mapOf(
    "method" to method,
    "attribute" to attribute,
    "values" to values,
).filterValues { it != null }

fun toJson(): String = toMap().toJson()
```

Realtime builds `RealtimeResponse`, `RealtimeResponseEvent`, and `AppwriteException` from the parsed map the same way. The cookie store reads and writes cookies with explicit keys that match the format earlier SDK versions wrote without R8, so sessions saved by those builds still load after you upgrade.

`toMap()` and `toJson()` are public on both `Query` and `Operator`, which helps when you want to log or inspect a query. Query keys also serialize in a fixed order now (`method`, `attribute`, `values`), where before the order depended on the runtime.

# Narrower keep rules and higher R8 scores

Version 29.0.0 also adds a `Push` service that receives push messages over MQTT without Firebase Cloud Messaging. It's built on the [HiveMQ MQTT client](https://github.com/hivemq/hivemq-mqtt-client), which uses Netty for networking. Netty reaches some of its own fields by name through atomic field updaters and `Unsafe` offsets, so it needs keep rules under R8.

The rules HiveMQ suggests for Android keep the name of every Netty member:

```
-keepclassmembernames class io.netty.** { *; }
-keepclassmembers class org.jctools.** { *; }
```

Those rules are safe, but they stop R8 from renaming any method in a large dependency. Shipped as consumer rules, they would have applied to every app using the SDK. Before release, the SDK team narrowed them to what Netty actually looks up by name: its fields, plus three methods its leak detector finds by name when its classes load (`toLeakAwareBuffer`, `recordLeakNonRefCountingOperation`, and `touch`).

```
-keepclassmembernames class com.hivemq.client.internal.shaded.io.netty.** { <fields>; }
-keepclassmembers class com.hivemq.client.internal.shaded.org.jctools.** { <fields>; }
```

The team measured the difference on a minimal app that depends on the SDK, built with R8 full mode (`proguard-android-optimize.txt`, AGP 9.4), with scores read from the same `r8.json` that Play uses:

| Metric | Blanket Netty rules | 29.0.0 rules |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Obfuscation score | 67.4% | 89.3% |
| Optimization score | 65.8% | 87.8% |
| Shrinking score | 95.0% | 95.1% |
| DEX size | 2.57 MB | 2.44 MB |

These numbers come from a minimal app, so your own scores depend on your code and your other dependencies. What the table shows is that the Appwrite SDK isn't the thing holding them down.

The SDK also depends on the shaded `hivemq-mqtt-client-shaded` artifact, which relocates Netty and JCTools into a single jar. The unshaded client pulls Netty in as several jars that each carry their own `META-INF/INDEX.LIST`, and Android's packager rejects the duplicates unless the app adds packaging excludes. With the shaded jar, your app needs no excludes for the SDK.

# R8 coverage in the SDK's test suite

Until this release, the SDK's end-to-end tests only ran against unshrunk code, which is how these bugs went unnoticed. The SDK now has an R8 test harness. It shrinks the SDK, its dependencies, and the test code with an app's R8 configuration, then runs the full end-to-end suite on the output. A second job keeps covering the unshrunk build.

Run against the SDK code from before the fix, the harness fails the same way apps did: queries emit `{}` and Realtime receives nothing. On 29.0.0, all 225 assertions pass, including push over MQTT. Every change to the Android SDK goes through both runs, so R8 regressions surface in CI instead of in your crash reports.

# How to use the Appwrite Android SDK with R8

## Update to 29.0.0

Update the dependency in your app-level `build.gradle.kts`:

```kotlin
implementation("io.appwrite:sdk-for-android:29.0.0")
```

Check two things before you upgrade:

- **The minimum SDK is now 24** (Android 7.0), up from 23.
- **The Push service merges manifest entries** into your app: Push permissions, a foreground service, and a boot receiver.

The [29.0.0 release notes](https://github.com/appwrite/sdk-for-android/releases/tag/29.0.0) list every other change, including new services and fixes for Android 7.x.

## Turn on R8 in your release build

On AGP 9.3 and later, use the `optimization` block:

```kotlin
android {
    buildTypes {
        release {
            optimization {
                enable = true
            }
        }
    }
}
```

On earlier AGP versions, set `isMinifyEnabled` and `isShrinkResources`:

```kotlin
android {
    buildTypes {
        release {
            isMinifyEnabled = true
            isShrinkResources = true
            proguardFiles(
                getDefaultProguardFile("proguard-android-optimize.txt"),
                "proguard-rules.pro",
            )
        }
    }
}
```

You don't need to add any Appwrite rules to `proguard-rules.pro`.

## Annotate your own model classes with `@Keep`

The SDK's own classes no longer depend on reflection, but yours might. When you pass a class as the `nestedType` of a document, row, or preferences call, or as the `payloadType` of a Realtime subscription, the SDK hands it to Gson to fill from JSON. R8 can't see that, so annotate those classes with `@Keep`:

```kotlin
import androidx.annotation.Keep

@Keep
data class Note(val title: String, val body: String)

val rows = tablesDB.listRows(
    databaseId = "<DATABASE_ID>",
    tableId = "<TABLE_ID>",
    queries = listOf(Query.equal("pinned", true)),
    nestedType = Note::class.java,
)
rows.rows.forEach { Log.d("Notes", it.data.title) }

val subscription = realtime.subscribe(
    Channel.tablesdb("<DATABASE_ID>").table("<TABLE_ID>").row(),
    payloadType = Note::class.java,
) { event ->
    Log.d("Notes", event.payload.title)
}
```

Without `@Keep`, the same R8 behavior that broke the SDK breaks your models: fields come back empty, or Gson throws when it tries to create the class. Limit `@Keep` to the classes the SDK fills from JSON so R8 can still optimize the rest of your app. Calls that omit `nestedType` return `Map<String, Any>` and need no annotation.

## Check your R8 scores

Read the numbers Play reads by extracting `r8.json` from your release bundle:

```bash
unzip -p app-release.aab BUNDLE-METADATA/com.android.tools/r8.json
```

Play Console shows the same percentages for each bundle you upload, in the app bundle explorer. If a score is lower than you expect, the [R8 Configuration Analyzer](https://developer.android.com/topic/performance/app-optimization/r8-configuration-analyzer) points to the keep rules responsible. Its scores reflect your keep rules before optimization, so they can differ from `r8.json`.

Then test the release build itself, not only debug. Sign in, install a second release build over the first, and confirm the session survives. Tests against a debug build never cover that path.

# Getting started with R8 and the Appwrite Android SDK

R8 is moving from optional to expected on Google Play, and your dependencies decide how much of your app R8 can actually optimize. With 29.0.0, the Appwrite Android SDK works in R8 full mode and stays out of the way of your scores, so you can turn R8 on without writing rules for it.

Create a project on [Appwrite Cloud](https://cloud.appwrite.io), add the SDK, and ship your next release with R8 on. Self-hosted Appwrite works the same way.

